[Battle Group 6.Name]
X Corps

[Battle Group 2.Name]
21st Ind. Marine Reg.

[USA.Epilogue]
The UN and South Korean forces were unified under a common goal – the destruction of the North Korean forces, and the rectification of borders. They were determined to push the North Koreans back across the northern borders of the Democratic People's Republic of Korea, and they were successful. It was an astonishing reversal, and could only have been accomplished with the full cooperation of ground, air, and naval forces, who delivered coordinated, simultaneous strikes to the front, flanks and enemy rears, with the support of numerous air and artillery units. Their personnel and military hardware superiority served them well, for the enemy lacked air defenses, artillery, and tanks.

[Battle Group 1.Name]


[USA.FailureEpilogue]
The unified offensive of the US and South Korean troops in Korea exposed a number of shortcomings in their formations and their units. UN Command forces proved unable to wage a sufficiently swift offensive, and combat exposed the inadequate readiness of the reinforcements. They had not been trained to fight at night or to use the mountainous terrain of Korea to their advantage. They used formations suitable mostly for plains, attacked across the entire battlefront, and limited their combat operations to the daylight hours. They did not attempt to sweep the enemy flanks, or use the mountains to their advantage. Their infantry could only fight when it was supported by artillery, tanks and air forces. Extensive use of roads and badly defended flanks and rears, as well as a general fear of encirclement – all of these shortcomings plagued the troops of UN Command.

